WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.170
From user interviews to a clickable prototype.

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.630
This is the video for module 2 of UX Design Fundamentals.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.240
In this module you move through the whole UX workflow on your own project.

4
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:20.840
You start by talking to 5 to 8 people who match your users. When you hear the same stories again and again, you have enough.

5
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.010
You turn your notes into themes, and count how many people said each thing.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.470
You map how she does the task today, and plan the screens for the new design.

7
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.900
Then you build simple wireframes and link them so people can tap through.

8
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.860
People are poor at predicting what they will do, but good at describing what they did.

9
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.800
Leading questions like these suggest the answer, so you only get a polite yes.

10
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:56.350
Open questions start with what, how, or tell me about. Tell me about the last time you booked a fitting gets the truth.

11
00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.370
Then listen more than you talk. People often add the most honest detail when they fill a pause.

12
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:15.870
For example, Blessing is redesigning bookings for a hair salon in Ikeja that takes bookings only on WhatsApp. She interviews 6 customers and writes 47 notes, one idea per note.

13
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:25.010
She groups the notes and names 5 themes, such as waiting for a reply, and price surprise. This is called affinity mapping.

14
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:35.470
Then she counts. 5 of 6 waited hours for the salon to confirm a slot. 4 of 6 learned the full price for braids only at the salon.

15
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.260
A theme 5 out of 6 people mention matters more than one mentioned once.

16
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:52.970
Finally, a one-page persona: goals, frustrations, device, habits and real quotes. A persona written before research is only a guess, so label it and replace it.

17
00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:04.800
A journey map shows the task over time. In the salon map, she waits 3 hours for a price, is unsure the booking is confirmed, and finds her stylist double-booked.

18
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.820
Those low points are where design helps most.

19
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:17.040
Card sorting shows how users group things. If they say hairstyles and the business says services, the menu should say hairstyles.

20
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:28.340
A user flow shows every screen and decision for one task. Draw the happy path first, then the error paths: slot taken, payment failed, network dropped.

21
00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:42.310
Here is the flow Blessing draws. Screen 1 shows styles and prices. Screen 2 shows only free slots. If a slot is just taken, she sees a sorry message and keeps her chosen style.

22
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:55.820
Screen 3 asks only for name and phone. Every extra field is a chance to give up. If the deposit payment fails, she sees the reason and a try again button without losing her booking.

23
00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:05.990
Screen 5 confirms the date, time, stylist, amount paid and a reference. Every pain point from the journey map now has an answer.

24
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:20.170
Next, Blessing builds two grey, mid-fidelity versions in Figma and asks 5 customers to book Saturday at 10am. With the month calendar, 3 of 5 scrolled past the slots and needed help.

25
00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:30.550
With the row of days, all 5 booked without help, in about 20 seconds. She keeps Version B, and moves Confirm lower for easier thumb reach.

26
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.720
She compared two ideas in one afternoon, before spending any money on development.

27
00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:49.430
Check every screen. Real labels like book appointment. One main action. The button where the thumb reaches. Big tap targets, about 44 to 48 units by platform guidelines.

28
00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:57.330
The common mistake is jumping to colours before the flow works, and forgetting what the screen shows when there are no free slots.

29
00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:12.280
To remember. Ask about real past behaviour. Count your themes. Design for the lowest points. Keep wireframes grey until the flow works, then link them in the Prototype tab and share the link for testing.

30
00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:22.080
Your turn. Interview, find your themes, and write your persona. Map the journey and the flow, sketch, and build your clickable prototype.

31
00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.370
Continue with the lessons below.
